# What the Vercel CLI uploads for a deploy. Separate from vercel.json's
# `excludeFiles` (which trims the built function bundle) — this trims what
# ever leaves this machine in the first place, which matters most for the
# things that must never leave it at all.

.venv/
data/
keys/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

.git/
.github/
.claude/
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

tests/
docs/
node_modules/

PLAN.md
README.md
PRD Topic Research to Publish Pipeline.md
railway.json
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Excluded deliberately, not an oversight: Vercel's Python build detects
# dependencies from requirements.txt OR pyproject.toml, and if both are
# present it isn't documented which wins. pyproject.toml's base
# `dependencies` list pulls in langgraph, fastembed (+ ~45MB onnxruntime),
# textstat, rich (already covered explicitly in requirements.txt) and typer —
# none reachable from any dashboard code path (traced import-by-import before
# writing requirements.txt). Excluding this file is what guarantees
# requirements.txt is the only manifest Vercel can see.
pyproject.toml

